  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiescent weather expected through the end of \nthe week.\n\nHigh pressure moves offshore today turning the winds out of the\nsouth. This will yield a milder day with temps climbing back \ninto the 70s. Temps warm up to near 80 Thu before the passage \nof another dry cold front. Temps cool off slightly for the end \nof the week as weak high pressure of Canadian origin builds in. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Increased rain potential late this weekend into\nearly next week. \n\nThe main feature to watch in the extended portion of the forecast \nwill be Tropical Storm Isaias. Key steering features will be a \nridge in the western Atlantic, a trough/closed low over Texas, \nand eventually a trough ejecting from the northern Rockies. The \nNHC forecast has the remnants of this system moving into the \nTennessee and Ohio Valleys over the weekend, although there \nstill remains considerable uncertainty on the track and \nespecially forward speed of the system. Even despite this \nwestward position of the cyclone, moisture from both the Gulf of\nAmerica and Atlantic will likely be transported northward into \nthe Mid Atlantic. Rain will become increasingly likely Sun, \nalthough as mentioned, this timing could vary a bit. The remnant\nlow could then move eastward, potentially extending rain \nchances into Monday. At this juncture, the rain will be the main\nimpact from this system, but we will continue to monitor any \nchanging trends.\n\nIt will be important to stay tuned to the forecast over the next few \ndays, and monitor hurricanes.gov for updated details on Tropical \nStorm Isaias.\n\nDaytime temperatures will likely be seasonal through the weekend and \nearly next week but will take a dip when rain is the most \nwidespread. Meanwhile overnight lows will be mild thanks to \nincreasing clouds and dew points.",
